<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huastec people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Teenek</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Huastec, or Teenek, are an Indigenous people of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> who speak a Mayan language that separated from the other Mayan languages long ago. They live in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huasteca">Huasteca</a> region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a>. Their pre-Hispanic culture was known for shell ornaments, distinctive pottery and stone sculpture, and much of their land was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> in the 15th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huastec-people.jpg" alt="Huastec people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tania Victoria / Secretaría de Cultura de la Ciudad de México · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MX_TV_TLACUATZIN_SON_HUASTECO.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Huastec, or Téenek, a name contracted from Te' Inik, meaning people from here, live in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huasteca">Huasteca</a>, which covers parts of Hidalgo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamaulipas">Tamaulipas</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panuco-river">Pánuco River</a> and the Gulf coast. Their language descends from Proto-Mayan, and proto-Huastecan was the first language to split from Mayan proper. The other language to arise from it was Chicomuceltec, once spoken near Comitán in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a> and now extinct. Linguists have estimated that the split took place between 2200 and 1200 BCE, with 2000 BCE a reasonable date, and the word for writing is different in proto-Huastec and in the other Maya languages.</p><p>According to this reconstruction, the Huastecs moved north from the Maya homeland in the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, probably along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usumacinta-river">Usumacinta River</a>, and picked up loan words from Mixe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoque">Zoque</a>, the language most scholars link to the Olmec. They are thought to have been pushed up the Gulf coast by the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmecs</a> of San Lorenzo, and they reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huasteca">Huasteca</a> between 1500 and 900 BCE. Richard Stockton MacNeish found Middle Formative ceramics and figurines at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panuco-river">Pánuco River</a> sites in 1954 that resemble objects from the Maya site of Uaxactun.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological remains date the Huastec civilization to about the 10th century BCE, and its most productive period is usually placed in the Postclassic, between the fall of Teotihuacan and the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>. The Huastecs built temples on step pyramids, carved free-standing sculpture and made elaborately painted pottery, and other Mesoamerican peoples admired their music. Their art drew on the coast and made much use of shell: gorgets, fan headdresses, heads and figures of hunchbacked humans. Around 1450 Aztec armies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-i">Moctezuma I</a> defeated them. They then paid tribute to the Aztec Empire but kept substantial local self-government.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> conquered the Huastecs between 1519 and the 1530s, and many were sold as slaves in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. The friar Andrés de Olmos wrote the first grammatical and lexical description of the Huastec language available to Europeans. Their population, perhaps as much as half a million when the Spanish arrived in 1529, was probably much higher than it is today.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>About 66,000 people speak Huastec today, two thirds of them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a> and one third in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huasteca">Huasteca</a> also holds speakers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> and Pame. In the late 19th century Huastec chicle-tappers and lumbermen were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a> to work similar forests there, in effect reuniting them with the Yucatán Maya for the first time in millennia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huastec people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Huastec</b> separated from other Maya speakers well over two thousand years ago and settled the lowlands of northern Veracruz, San Luis Potosí and Tamaulipas.</blockquote><a href="/state/huastec-chiefdoms">Huastec chiefdoms</a></li></ul></section>
